# CLUTCH-X

CLUTCH-X analyzes delivery-level T20 match data to identify reliable batting and
bowling performances under pressure. The project is organized as a Python
analytics engine, a React dashboard, and a reproducible report generator.

## Data

Place the six source CSVs in `Data Pack/` (or provide another directory with
`--data-dir`):

- `ball_by_ball.csv`
- `matches.csv`
- `competitions.csv`
- `playing_eleven.csv`
- `teams.csv`
- `competition_team_player_mapping.csv`

The supplied pack has 200,999 deliveries, 883 matches, and competition records
from 2022 through 2026. Its `T20` format includes IPL and non-IPL competitions;
the pipeline uses competition name and format to retain IPL and T20I while
excluding non-IPL T20 competitions.

## Getting started

Use Python 3.13 as specified in the architecture document. Python 3.11–3.13
are accepted by the current package metadata to support the provided
development environment. After installing the project dependencies:

```sh
python3 -m venv .venv
. .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
python -m clutch_x.etl.validate_sources --data-dir "Data Pack"
python -m clutch_x.etl.reconstruct_state --data-dir "Data Pack" --output-dir data/processed
```

Validation reports malformed inputs and broken relationships before
processing. The state pipeline scopes to IPL/T20I, then excludes Super Over
innings and abandoned/no-result matches with fewer than 30 legal deliveries in
total. Original CSV files are read-only inputs and are not rewritten. It writes
`data/processed/delivery_states.parquet`.

## Source data contracts

The loader preserves the source columns and adds normalized typed columns.
Foreign-key joins are validated before the transformed delivery artifact is
written.

| File | Row grain and key | Relationships |
|---|---|---|
| `ball_by_ball.csv` | One delivery event; `(match_id, innings, innings_ball_num)` | `match_id` → matches |
| `matches.csv` | One match; `match_id` | `comp_id` → competitions |
| `competitions.csv` | One competition; `comp_id` | Format must be `T20` or `T20I` |
| `playing_eleven.csv` | One player/batting-position record; `(match_id, team_id, player_id, batting_order)` | `match_id` → matches; batting order and some role flags may be blank |
| `teams.csv` | One competition team; `(comp_id, team_id)` | `comp_id` → competitions |
| `competition_team_player_mapping.csv` | One competition/team/player; `(comp_id, team_id, player_id)` | `comp_id` → competitions |

Roster records may contain the same player at multiple batting positions,
including across Super Overs, so player identity alone is not unique in that
file. The delivery source uses `extras_type=wide` and `Overstep` for illegal
balls; its `other` label also consistently carries a one-run penalty and is
treated as a no-ball for legal-ball counts. Revised-overs fields are consumed
as legal-ball limits (for example, `114` means 114 legal balls). The normal T20
limit defaults to 120 legal balls.

The [`prd.md`](prd.md), [`design.md`](design.md), and [`techStack.md`](techStack.md)
documents define the product, visual system, and intended architecture.

## Initial Pressure Index specification

The current deterministic PI implementation computes the format/season par run
rate as the mean first-innings run rate across matches. For a chase, it combines
a nonlinear rate-tension score (`100 × clip(RRR / par_rate − 1, 0, 1)²`) at
75% with the wicket/resource interaction (`wickets_lost / 9 ×
(1 − balls_remaining / scheduled_balls)`) at 25%. First-innings pressure is
the greater of the collapse score (70 at three early wickets, increasing with
further wickets; 75 at five wickets before over 15) and death-over urgency
(30 at over 16, increasing by 15 per over). Knockout pressure is multiplied by
1.15, then clamped to 0–100. PI 60 is the high-pressure cutoff; 80 is the
critical-leverage label.

Run `python -m clutch_x.etl.score_pressure` after state reconstruction to
produce `data/processed/pressure_deliveries.parquet`. With the supplied data,
the initial specification classifies 16.99% of deliveries as high pressure,
within the PRD's 15–25% target. Verify the acceptance gates with
`python -m clutch_x.etl.validate_pressure`: the current data shows an 8.0%
high-pressure wicket rate versus 4.88% in normal pressure (chi-square
`p < 0.01`) and a statistically different run variance (Levene `p < 0.01`).

Run `python -m clutch_x.etl.normalizer` to attach format/season/innings/phase
expected runs and wicket probability. Sparse bucket estimates are shrunk toward
the corresponding format/innings/phase mean using 30 prior deliveries.
`python -m clutch_x.etl.baselines` and `python -m clutch_x.etl.score_batting`
then create personal normal-pressure profiles, batter pressure deltas, and
match-level impact audit rows. `python -m clutch_x.etl.score_bowling` generates
bowling economy/dot deltas, pressure runs saved per over, credited wickets
(with top-order wickets weighted 1.5× for impact), and match-level audit rows.
`python -m clutch_x.etl.reliability_filter` applies the PRD's 150-ball/10-match
eligibility floor, shrinks per-ball/over impact toward the eligible peer mean
with a 150-ball prior, and penalizes match-to-match volatility as `mean −
0.5 × sample_std`.

`python -m clutch_x.etl.rank_players` exports the two Top-10 artifacts. The
leaderboard first percentile-ranks each component within its eligible
discipline cohort. Baseline ratings weight normal strike rate/economy at 60%,
with boundary/dot/wicket rates making up the rest. Pressure ratings weight
volatility-adjusted pRAE/pRSAE at 40%, baseline deltas at 25%, and the remaining
pressure diagnostics at 35%. The final score is 55% pressure rating, 25% of the
baseline-adjusted delta mapped to 0–100, and 20% positive-impact consistency.
Ties break by pressure rating, net clutch delta, sample volume, then ascending
player ID. All weights and tie-breakers are explicit and deterministic.

Run `python -m clutch_x.etl.ranking_audit` to verify Top-10 rank integrity,
summarize high-pressure exposure by competition and innings, and measure
odd/even chronological match split-half Spearman stability for eligible
players. The supplied data passes the Top-10 integrity checks, but split-half
stability is below the PRD's 0.65 target: batting rho is 0.1985 (47 eligible
players) and bowling rho is 0.3317 (68 eligible players). The audit exits
unsuccessfully on this acceptance-gate miss and still writes the exposure
artifacts. These rankings should therefore be treated as exploratory, not as
validated stable rankings; the threshold has not been weakened to conceal the
observed result.

After generating the processed Parquet artifacts, run
`python -m clutch_x.etl.build_store` to materialize them as DuckDB tables in
`data/processed/clutch_x.duckdb`. The database includes an artifact catalog,
store schema version, SHA-256/size manifests for the six input CSVs, and
checksums for the ETL configuration modules used to produce the artifacts.

The API query layer reads from DuckDB with parameterized competition, innings,
stage, and over filters. Filtered leaderboards, comparisons, and scatter
cohorts are recalculated through the same normalization, reliability, and
ranking functions as the batch pipeline; pressure timelines aggregate the
delivery table by over.

Start the API with `python -m uvicorn clutch_x.api.main:app --host 127.0.0.1
--port 8000`; set `CLUTCH_X_DB` to use a different DuckDB file. The versioned
routes provide overview metadata, filter options, filtered Top-10 leaderboards,
innings timelines, quadrant cohorts, up-to-three-player comparisons, and
per-player match audit records. Invalid filters and ineligible player
selections return explicit 422 responses; an unavailable database returns
503.

## Methodology report

After building the verified Top-10, exposure, and ranking-audit artifacts,
install the Typst CLI and run:

```sh
python -m clutch_x.report.build_report
```

The report builder validates both ordered Top-10 artifacts, computes the
pressure-regime checks from normalized deliveries, and compiles
`reports/clutch_x_methodology.pdf`. The matching `report-data.json` snapshot
and compiled Typst source are emitted beside the PDF. The report includes the
formulas and thresholds, PI distribution, pressure and stability audits,
rankings, exposure summaries, artifact/source checksums, and explicit
limitations. A missed stability target is preserved in the report rather than
hidden or treated as a build failure.

## Continuous integration

GitHub Actions runs the Python tests, frontend production build and npm audit,
then rebuilds the analytics artifacts from the six source CSVs and enforces
the pressure and split-half statistical gates. The analytics job still builds
the store and report when the ranking audit misses its gate, then returns the
audit failure so the limitation remains visible in CI. A Docker image build
also runs from the repository root.

## Docker image

The multi-stage Docker build installs the API and runs the deterministic ETL
against `Data Pack/`, then copies only the generated DuckDB database into the
runtime image. The source CSVs and intermediate Parquet files remain in the
builder stage. Build and run the API with:

```sh
docker build -t clutch-x-api:local .
docker run --rm -p 8000:8000 clutch-x-api:local
```

The container exposes FastAPI on port 8000 and uses
`CLUTCH_X_DB=/data/clutch_x.duckdb`. Its ranking audit explicitly allows the
known split-half stability miss so exploratory analytics remain available;
the GitHub Actions analytics gate still fails that acceptance criterion.

See [docs/DEPLOYMENT.md](docs/DEPLOYMENT.md) for the selected Cloudflare Pages
and Railway deployment configuration, release steps, and operational limits.
